     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi, <br><br>I am quite new to fitting models with categorial variables and therefor would really appreciate some help.<br><br>I ran a model with a binary endogenous variable and the results look suspicious to me.<br><br>Here is the code I used:<br><br>KINDEP2 &lt;- &#39;#measurement model<br>            PE =~ apk1 + apk2 + apk6 + apk9 + apk13 + apk16 + apk23 + apk25 + apk29 + apk33<br>                  + apk34 + apk37 + apk40 + apk42 + apk45 + apk47 + apk49<br>            DEP =~ deq02 + deq19 + deq23 + deq50 + deq55<br>            SC =~ deq07 + deq13 + deq30 + deq36 + deq53 + deq64 + deq66<br><br>            #structural model<br>            DEP ~ PE <br>            SC ~ PE <br>            Gruppe ~ DEP + SC + PE<br><br>            #cov<br>            DEP ~~ SC<br>            &#39;<br><br>fit &lt;- sem(KINDEP2, data = KINDEP, ordered = &quot;Gruppe&quot;)<br>summary(fit, standardized = TRUE)<br>fitMeasures(fit, c(&quot;cfi&quot;, &quot;rmsea&quot;, &quot;srmr&quot;))<br><br>My console prints this (please see below):<br><br>- How is it possible to get a p-value of 1?<br>- How is it possible to get a CFI estimation of 1 and RMSEA = 0, while SRMR seems to provide a reasonable estimation?<br>- Also: can anybody recommend a book or paper on fitting categorial models in SEM?<br><br>Thanks a lot in advance!<br>Best,<br>Simone<br><br><pre style=\"font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;font-size:10pt!important;outline:none;border:none;word-break:break-all;margin:0px;white-space:pre-wrap!important;line-height:15px;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:-webkit-left;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\">lavaan (0.5-20) converged normally after  76 iterations\r\n\r\n  Number of observations                            90\r\n\r\n  Estimator                                       DWLS      Robust\r\n  Minimum Function Test Statistic              