  {
   "id": "JUYISBx1AwAJ",
   "from": "Terrence Jorgensen",
   "author": "3fe90052fbf2f899",
   "date": "2016-08-26T11:44:34Z",
   "subject": "Re: mediation model with binary endogenous variable",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">- How is it possible to get a p-value of 1?<br></div></blockquote><div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><i>p</i> values can approach (i.e., round up to) 1 when most of the sampling distribution is more extreme than the observed statistic.</div><div><br></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">- How is it possible to get a CFI estimation of 1 and RMSEA = 0, while SRMR seems to provide a reasonable estimation?</div></blockquote><div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div>You want to pay attention to the &quot;robust&quot; column.  The corresponding chi-squared-based fit indices have a &quot;<font face=\"courier new, monospace\">.scaled</font>&quot; suffix.</div><blockquote style=\"border:none;padding:0px\"><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">fitMeasures(fit, c(&quot;cfi.scaled&quot;, &quot;rmsea.scaled&quot;, &quot;srmr&quot;))</font></blockquote></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\"><br></font></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">- Also: can anybody recommend a book or paper on fitting categorial models in SEM?<br></div></blockquote><div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px\">Hoyle&#39;s (2012) <i>Handbook of Structural Equation Modeling</i> has a couple chapters dealing with categorical endogenous variables.  Here is a Jöreskog paper you might find helpful:</div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px\"><br></div><div><a href=\"http://dx.doi.org/10.1207/S15327906347-387\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://dx.doi.org/10.1207/S15327906347-387&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792428067448000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1NivCiOXLvtqB_qLvJwZKC\">http://dx.doi.org/10.1207/S15327906347-387</a><br></div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px\"><br></div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px\">You can also post general SEM questions like this on SEMNET:</div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px\"><br></div><div><a href=\"http://www2.gsu.edu/~mkteer/semnet.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www2.gsu.edu/~mkteer/semnet.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792428067448000&amp;usg=AOvVaw39xVhmR5_agnqW1VL_J-44\">www2.gsu.edu/~mkteer/semnet.html</a><br></div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px\"><br></div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px\">Terrence D. Jorgensen</div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px\">Postdoctoral Researcher, Methods and Statistics</div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px\">Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam</div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px\">UvA web page: <a href=\"http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen\" style=\"color:rgb(17,85,204)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792428067448000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1Nr1LFikfhSBmfGlvFsyWc\">http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen</a></div><div style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small\"><br></div></div></div></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
